# SYRI and other such SV detecting programs can be used to carryout a 'crude' version of measuring SVs between species
# This uses SV counts rather than landmarking genomic regions to measure the amount of SVs, so we do not recommend this pipeline
# However, to get an idea of the 'number' of SVs accumulated across different species pairs, one could use the following script to filter and carryout an MDS

# Assumes users have already run SYRI, see SYRI's github for how to run SYRI (https://github.com/schneebergerlab/syri)

# SYRI identifies SVs and stores output in a vcf format that also contains information on Single Nucleotide Polymorphisms (SNPs), Syntenic regions (SYNs), and highly diverged regions (HDRs) as it works on pairwise genome alignments
# None of these are true SVs, and therefore we need to filter these out from the vcf files

#each pairwise comparison produces a vcf file, so do this for all the vcf files

# CAVEATS
# pairwise comparisons
# subject to evolutionary divergence between species being compared (close species align better, they could have more 'fine-scale' SVs than species that do not align very well)
# Using SV counts only 

